---
name: conformance-js-compound-assign
---

# `x += 1` is compiled, not looked up

On the JS lane a compound assignment was emitted as `_dispatch(i, '+=', [1])` — a dynamic method
lookup for a method literally named `+=`. `_dispatch` has no such entry, so every program using
`+=`/`-=`/`*=` threw `Method not found: += on 0`. The operator was not compiled at all, it was
looked UP. It took down the entire `js` benchmark column, because the bench wrapper itself uses `+=`.

The rewrite already existed in the codegen — and was reachable only from the for-comprehension
GENERATOR path, so an ordinary statement fell through to the generic infix arm. That is why the last
two rows are here: they exercise the position that already worked, so a future change cannot fix one
position by breaking the other.

`:=` is deliberately NOT exercised as a compound assignment: it is a DSL assign operator in its own
right (`attr.x := v`), and widening the rewrite made it reachable for the first time — it is
excluded in the codegen rather than left to chance.

```scalascript
def sumTo(n: Int): Int =
  var acc = 0
  var i = 1
  while i <= n do
    acc += i
    i += 1
  acc

def main() =
  var i = 0
  i += 1
  i += 1
  println(i)

  var s = 10
  s -= 3
  println(s)

  var p = 4
  p *= 5
  println(p)

  var t = "a"
  t += "b"
  println(t)

  // the while-loop shape the bench wrapper uses
  println(sumTo(4))

  // a for-comprehension generator — the ONE position that already worked
  var g = 0
  for k <- 1 to 3 do g += k
  println(g)
```
